     ments.  You must use the --mm or --MM options if there are multiple input
     files.  You can specify input from stdin or output to stdout with ‘-’.

     The uunniiffddeeff utility works nicely with the --DD_s_y_m option of diff(1).

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
     In normal usage the uunniiffddeeff utility's exit status depends on the mode set
     using the --xx option.

     If the exit mode is zero (the default) then uunniiffddeeff exits with status 0
     if the output is an exact copy of the input, or with status 1 if the out‐
     put differs.

     If the exit mode is one, uunniiffddeeff exits with status 1 if the output is un‐
     modified or 0 if it differs.

     If the exit mode is two, uunniiffddeeff exits with status zero in both cases.

     In all exit modes, uunniiffddeeff exits with status 2 if there is an error.

     The exit status is 0 if the --hh or --VV command line options are given.

